Hi, I want to plot a bar chart using a csv file on my desktop.

If I call the data with "read.csv", and keep the df in memory my plots get executed. However, I don't know how to use the same data from the upload file widget.

I've followed this example: http://shiny.rstudio.com/gallery/upload-file.html and was able to plot a data table.

But how can I plot a barchart using the data frame uploaded? How do I access it?

## server.R ###

output$contents <- renderTable({

              inFile <- input$file1

              if (is.null(inFile))
                return(NULL)

              read.csv(inFile$datapath, header = input$header,
                       sep = input$sep, quote = input$quote)

              })

This is how I do plot a data table. But how to access the new df to use it in another graphics?

I've tried this (but got: object 'inFile' not found) :

######################################

output$contents <- renderTable({

              inFile <- input$file1

              if (is.null(inFile))
                return(NULL)

              inFile <- read.csv(inFile$datapath, header = input$header,
                       sep = input$sep, quote = input$quote)

              })

####################################

        output$testPlot <- renderChart2({

          inFile2 <- inFile[inFile$date >= input$dates[1]
                                                    & inFile$date <= input$dates[2], ]

          inFile2 <- inFile2[inFile2$fuente %in% input$show_vars, ]

          inFile2 <- inFile2 %>%
                        group_by(fuente) %>%
                        summarise(sessions = sum(sessions))

                h1 <- hPlot(x = "fuente", y = "sessions", data = inFile2, type = c("column"))

                h1$tooltip(formatter="#!function () {
                           return 'Sesiones '  +
                           ': <b>' + this.y + '</b>' +  ' (' + this.x + ')' ;
        }!#")

                h1$title(text = "Sesiones por fuente")
                h1$exporting(enabled=TRUE)

                return(h1)

        })

###########################################################

If you use:

usertypexfuente <- read.csv("your/path/usertypexfuente.csv")

You can see the graphs I've made with the data, from calling the data directly with read.csv.

